WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:05.705
I have attempted to relate in detail the affairs aboard the Provence thus far. In light of

2
00:00:05.706 --> 00:00:15.302
the circumstances, I was not surprised that, after Lady Jerland’s jewels were stolen, all eyes shifted to Monsieur

3
00:00:15.303 --> 00:00:22.094
Rozaine. Everyone thought the captain would detain Rozaine (who surely had to be Lupin) for the remainder

4
00:00:22.095 --> 00:00:28.015
of the voyage. But to our amazement, the next day Rozaine was walking around a free man.

5
00:00:28.016 --> 00:00:32.517
"What do you mean he’s been released?" barked Lady Jerland. "His documents show that he is who

6
00:00:32.518 --> 00:00:43.675
he says he is," I explained. "And he did not have a wound on his right arm. The

7
00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:51.016
captain had no evidence to arrest him." "Documents!" exclaimed Lady Jerland. "Lupin could furnish as many documents

8
00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:58.422
as might be desired!" Soon after the theft, Rozaine announced a reward of ten thousand francs for

9
00:00:58.423 --> 00:01:04.238
the capture of Lupin. "If no one assists me," declared Rozaine, "I will find the scoundrel myself."

10
00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:16.817
"Ah, Rozaine against Arsène Lupin, or rather, according to popular opinion, Arsène Lupin against himself," I thought. "The

11
00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:28.048
contest promises to be interesting." The captain and crew displayed admirable tenacity as they ransacked every cabin,

12
00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:37.794
leaving no stone unturned. The jewels had to be concealed somewhere aboard ship. All was quiet for

13
00:01:37.795 --> 00:01:49.794
the next few days. Miss Nelly and I watched as Rozaine searched everywhere, even in the life

14
00:01:49.795 --> 00:02:00.453
jackets stored under the lifeboats. "What does he hope to find?" whispered Miss Nelly. "Does Lupin have magical

15
00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:05.119
powers to shrink himself to the size of a rat? Or would he be foolish enough to

16
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.429
hide the jewels in plain view?" "If I were Rozaine, I would examine the lining of our

17
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:15.429
hats, the hems of skirts, and everything we carry with us," I said, holding up my camera

18
00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.850
as an example. "Within a device as compact as this, a person could hide jewels. He could pretend

19
00:02:20.851 --> 00:02:24.008
to take pictures and nobody would suspect a thing." "But even the cleverest of thieves must leave

20
00:02:24.009 --> 00:02:30.588
some clue behind," Miss Nelly insisted. "Generally speaking, perhaps," I replied. "But with one exception: Lupin." "Why

21
00:02:30.589 --> 00:02:38.580
must he be excluded?" demanded Miss Nelly. "Because most thieves concentrate on the theft and carelessly leave behind

22
00:02:38.581 --> 00:02:43.739
some trace of themselves. Lupin, however, focuses on all the circumstances that could somehow serve as a

23
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:50.826
clue to his identity," I explained. "That is why no one has been able to catch him.

24
00:02:50.827 --> 00:02:57.239
They don’t know who he is!" "Earlier you were so confident that you could discover the identity

25
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.072
of Lupin, Monsieur d’Andrézy," Miss Nelly remarked. "Indeed, I was," I admitted, "but since I have seen him

26
00:03:02.073 --> 00:03:09.731
at work, I think we are wasting our time." The investigation into Lady Jerland’s stolen jewels resulted

27
00:03:09.732 --> 00:03:17.890
in disappointment. In the meantime the captain’s watch was stolen too. Furious, the captain once again turned

28
00:03:17.891 --> 00:03:26.128
his attention to Rozaine. The next evening, however, Rozaine’s claim of innocence was justified. An officer on

29
00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:33.612
deck heard groans coming from under the lifeboats. He found Rozaine tied up, his head wrapped in a

30
00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:38.358
scarf, and with a card that revealed the perpetrator pinned to his coat. It read: Arsène Lupin

31
00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:44.279
gladly accepts the ten thousand francs offered by Monsieur Rozaine. As for the captain, he should ask

32
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:54.937
his second officer for the time of day. Rozaine’s wallet was empty, and the captain later found

33
00:03:54.938 --> 00:04:03.683
his watch inside the officer’s quarters. Arsène Lupin was now anybody and everybody. Fear permeated the ship as

34
00:04:03.684 --> 00:04:09.850
people prayed for the remainder of the voyage to pass without further incident. I, on the other

35
00:04:09.851 --> 00:04:18.493
hand, was probably the only passenger to benefit from the near-panic on the ship. It brought Miss

36
00:04:18.494 --> 00:04:24.334
Nelly and me closer together. She was afraid and mistrusted others, so I availed myself of her companionship

37
00:04:24.335 --> 00:04:30.414
throughout the ordeal. Miss Nelly and I shared many delightful hours together. She spoke fondly of her

38
00:04:30.415 --> 00:04:34.493
world travels, and I took photos of her with my camera. And now we waited anxiously as

39
00:04:34.494 --> 00:04:41.477
the Provence approached the shores of America. "Monsieur d’Andrézy," Miss Nelly cried breathlessly, "it appears we have

40
00:04:41.478 --> 00:04:49.056
a welcoming committee." She pointed to a group of uniformed officers standing on the dock. As the Provence

41
00:04:49.057 --> 00:04:53.723
pulled in, the officers separated to allow one man to walk through. I gasped. "You are pale,

42
00:04:53.724 --> 00:04:58.477
monsieur." Miss Nelly grabbed my right arm. I flinched but managed to suppress a cry of pain.

43
00:04:58.478 --> 00:05:05.120
"Do you see that little man?" I pointed and then handed my camera to her. "The one

44
00:05:05.121 --> 00:05:07.795
wearing the green coat and carrying an umbrella?" said Miss Nelly. "That very one. He is the famous

45
00:05:07.796 --> 00:05:16.049
Detective Ganimard," I replied. "Ganimard?" repeated Miss Nelly. "Yes, the detective who has dedicated his life to

46
00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:20.374
capturing Arsène Lupin," I continued. "That can mean only one thing . . ." "He will arrest

47
00:05:20.375 --> 00:05:27.930
Lupin?" Miss Nelly asked excitedly. I shrugged. "No doubt Lupin already knows his enemy is here. He

48
00:05:27.931 --> 00:05:34.946
will be in no hurry to leave the ship." The passengers walked down the gangway, but Ganimard appeared

49
00:05:34.947 --> 00:05:38.279
to pay no attention to the crowd disembarking from the ship. Even Rozaine walked off without a

50
00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:44.914
glance from the detective. "Who could Lupin be?" Miss Nelly wondered aloud, scrutinizing the few faces that

51
00:05:44.915 --> 00:05:49.176
remained on deck. The captain signaled for Miss Nelly and me to walk down the gangway next.

52
00:05:49.177 --> 00:05:54.097
We had not taken more than ten steps when Ganimard blocked our way. "A moment, please," he said.

53
00:05:54.098 --> 00:05:56.414
"What is the meaning of this, officer?" I demanded. "You know well what the meaning is"—here Ganimard

54
00:05:56.415 --> 00:05:59.509
paused—"Monsieur Lupin." I laughed loudly. "You are mistaken, sir. I am Bernard d’Andrézy. I believe Lupin was

55
00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:06.827
sailing under a name beginning with R ." Ganimard smiled coldly. "That telegram was a clever trick. It

56
00:06:06.828 --> 00:06:17.233
deceived everyone but me. I am well acquainted with your tactics, Lupin, and now your time is

57
00:06:17.234 --> 00:06:31.059
up." "Stop," Miss Nelly said. "Monsieur d’Andrézy is a gentleman." "Bernard d’Andrézy died three years ago," said

58
00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:36.297
Ganimard, and he raised his umbrella and struck my right arm with it. I cried out in

59
00:06:36.298 --> 00:06:44.805
pain. The wound had not yet healed and began to bleed again. I had no choice but to

60
00:06:44.806 --> 00:06:53.178
surrender. "Yes, I am the man you are looking for. However, what evidence do you have that

61
00:06:53.179 --> 00:07:00.265
I committed any crime?" I turned to Miss Nelly. Our eyes locked, and she glanced at the

62
00:07:00.266 --> 00:07:06.448
camera in her hand. She now understood what was inside the camera. "I am confident we will

63
00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:09.861
find what we are looking for," Ganimard said, his face close to mine. Miss Nelly walked past the

64
00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:16.249
detective and me, and then suddenly she seemed to trip. Faking awkwardness, she let the camera fall

65
00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:21.011
into the water between the ship and the pier. Then she walked down the gangway and was

66
00:07:21.012 --> 00:07:26.511
quickly lost in the crowd. She had passed out of my life forever. I turned to Ganimard

67
00:07:26.512 --> 00:07:33.805
and said, "It is indeed a shame that I am not an honest man." This is the story

68
00:07:33.806 --> 00:07:39.495
Arsène Lupin told me of his arrest. We are friends, and he occasionally comes to see me,

69
00:07:39.496 --> 00:07:49.074
bringing his youthful spirit with him. I have seen him twenty times, and each time, he is

70
00:07:49.075 --> 00:07:54.653
a different person. As I sit here organizing my notes, I plan to one day publish the

71
00:07:54.654 --> 00:08:00.963
affairs of this gentleman burglar. I hope the public will be able to refer to my work and

72
00:08:00.964 --> 00:08:10.881
say: "Arsène Lupin did that!" Now let me share with you what happened to Lupin in prison.

